{"name":"Emerald Spring","hex":"#095155","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"emerald","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various green gemstones, especially a green transparent form of beryl, highly valued as a precious stone.","Emerald green, a colour."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a rich green colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/emerald"},{"term":"spring","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To move or burst forth.","To appear."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An act of springing: a leap, a jump.","The season of the year in temperate regions in which temperatures and daylight hours rise, and plants spring from the ground and into bloom and dormant animals spring to life."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/spring"}],"entries":[{"title":"Emerald Spring","blocks":[{"text":"Emerald Spring is a hot spring located in Norris Geyser Basin of Yellowstone National Park."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/NorrisGeyserBasin-Emerald_Spring.JPG/330px-NorrisGeyserBasin-Emerald_Spring.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emerald_Spring","wikidata":"Q5370483"},{"title":"Emerald","blocks":[{"text":"Emerald is a gemstone and a variety of the mineral beryl (Be3Al2(SiO3)6) colored green by trace amounts of chromium or sometimes vanadium. Beryl has a hardness of 7.5–8 on the Mohs scale. Most emeralds have many inclusions, so their toughness is classified as poor. Emerald is a cyclosilicate. It occurs mainly in association with quartz, muscovite, albite, mica schist, Pyrite schorl, microcline, fluorite, smoky quartz and elbaite."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/B%C3%A9ryl_var._%C3%A9meraude_sur_gangue_%28Muzo_Mine_Boyaca_-_Colombie%29_2.jpg/330px-B%C3%A9ryl_var._%C3%A9meraude_sur_gangue_%28Muzo_Mine_Boyaca_-_Colombie%29_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedi